WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.960
The "Anchorman" movies are already&nbsp;
hilarious — but the bloopers amp the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:09.300
funny up to 11. From improvised condom names to&nbsp;
Ron's sexual fascination with Mrs. Butterworth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:14.306
these laugh-out-loud outtakes prove that sometimes&nbsp;
the funniest moments don't make the final cut.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:19.620
Diving into a character and delivering hours upon&nbsp;
hours of ad libbing is a near-impossible feat for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:23.700
many actors. However, Will Ferrell naturally&nbsp;
adopted the overly arrogant characteristics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:27.798
of news anchor Ron Burgundy from the&nbsp;
moment he put on the three-piece suit.

00:00:27.798 --> 00:00:31.861
"Hey, everyone! Come see how good I look."

00:00:31.861 --> 00:00:35.580
One of the best examples of Ferrell's&nbsp;
aptitude for the character occurs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:38.760
when he is left to his own devices.&nbsp;
Before the evening news camera roles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:43.440
Burgundy often warms up his vocal cords with&nbsp;
original tongue twisters or obnoxious screaming.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:51.267
"Ho ho! Ho ho ho! Hooooooo!&nbsp;
Hooo-hoooo! Ho-hooooo!"

00:00:51.267 --> 00:00:54.900
However,&nbsp;the deleted scenes showcase how&nbsp;
one of these lines was too silly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:59.160
even for Ferrell. While repeating a line about&nbsp;
the human torch being denied a bank loan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:02.554
Ferrell and the behind-the-scenes&nbsp;
crew were doubled over in laughter.
 &nbsp;

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.000
Ferrell and the rest of the onscreen&nbsp;
performers deserve most of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.720
credit for making the "Anchorman" movies&nbsp;
monumental comedic achievements. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:14.234
director Adam McKay is just as responsible&nbsp;
for a significant portion of the hilarity.

00:01:14.234 --> 00:01:17.294
McKay co-wrote the script for both&nbsp;
"Anchorman" movies with Ferrell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.294 --> 00:01:21.900
and his humor is peppered throughout the stories.&nbsp;
However, the director had just as much influence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:26.340
on the improvisational humor as some of the stars.&nbsp;
Throughout many of the bloopers and outtakes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:30.960
McKay can be heard hollering lines and jokes from&nbsp;
behind the camera. The best example of McKay's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:34.800
cleverness comes in an "Anchorman 2" scene.&nbsp;
In the scene, Ferrell is discussing breast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:39.780
implants with co-star Paul Rudd, and cracks that&nbsp;
they now use taco meat as the filler. But when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:43.440
McKay suggests "Nickels" from off-screen,&nbsp;
both Rudd and Ferrell have a quick laugh.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.500
Of course, even an anchorman of Ron Burgundy's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:49.920
caliber wouldn't be anywhere without&nbsp;
a superstar news team. Thankfully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:54.060
the movie did an outstanding job of filling&nbsp;
out the crew behind the Channel 4 News. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:58.216
diverse group of funnymen included David&nbsp;
Koechner, Paul Rudd, and Steve Carell.

00:01:58.216 --> 00:02:02.220
While fans got to know the entire Channel 4&nbsp;
News team well over the course of two films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:06.360
many deleted scenes helped audiences&nbsp;
learn even more about them. Much of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:09.360
the content deleted from the original&nbsp;
"Anchorman" film was collected into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:13.601
the direct-to-video counterpart film&nbsp;
"Wake Up, Ron Burgundy: The Lost Movie."

00:02:13.601 --> 00:02:17.220
However, the blooper reel for the first&nbsp;
"Anchorman" showcases a collection of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:21.900
unused taglines by the four seasoned stars&nbsp;
of San Diego's favorite news. Filmed in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:25.620
the same tone as a short TV commercial,&nbsp;
the Channel 4 News crew delivers a quick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:29.580
line that perfectly encapsulates their&nbsp;
characters. Burgundy fittingly says:

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:32.634
"Nothing like a good strong lady and a tasty BLT."

00:02:32.634 --> 00:02:36.900
Meanwhile, Carell's not-so-bright weatherman&nbsp;
character Brick nails it on the head saying,

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.794
"When there is weather to&nbsp;
report, I report the weather."
 &nbsp;

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:44.040
While the first "Anchorman's" assortment of&nbsp;
comedians helped make the movie a success,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:47.700
"Anchorman 2" brought in so many well-known&nbsp;
actors that the film struggled to fit them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:52.080
all in. Cleverly, the sequel doubled down on&nbsp;
a famous scene from the first movie where the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:56.400
Channel 4 News team got into a tussle with a&nbsp;
rival group of anchors. However, in "Anchorman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:00.900
2," the fight got much bigger, involving news&nbsp;
teams from all over the world. Furthermore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:05.820
each group features some of the biggest names in&nbsp;
Hollywood, including Will Smith and Jim Carrey.

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:10.680
With so many funny people in one place, shooting&nbsp;
the fight scene was understandably a rambunctious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:15.360
occasion. And the "Anchorman 2" bloopers do&nbsp;
not disappoint in showing off the chaos. One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:19.224
outtake shows how difficult it was for Carrey&nbsp;
to remember his Canadian news anchor name.

00:03:19.224 --> 00:03:21.204
"Without… what the hell's my name?"

00:03:21.204 --> 00:03:24.720
The entire event must have been too&nbsp;
much for everyone because even comedy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:27.960
veteran Will Smith wound up crying&nbsp;
— probably from laughing so hard.
 &nbsp;

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:32.640
Filming a movie as hilarious as "Anchorman"&nbsp;
without laughing sounds like an insurmountable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:37.080
job. So it's no wonder that the majority of&nbsp;
the outtakes and bloopers on this list feature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:40.740
someone bursting into laughter. However,&nbsp;
a few scenes went off the rails without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:45.984
giggling — most notably, a deep conversation Ron&nbsp;
Burgundy had with his beloved pet dog Baxter.

00:03:45.984 --> 00:03:47.530
In the scene from the first movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:51.180
Ferrell's character is greeted by his&nbsp;
loyal canine after returning home from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:55.560
work. When Burgundy tells Baxter about his&nbsp;
love life in an extended outtake, he says:

00:03:55.560 --> 00:04:01.856
"I think it was Aristotle who said: Come&nbsp;
children. Let me tell you a tale about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.856 --> 00:04:04.185
ships and whales."

00:04:04.185 --> 00:04:05.820
In an unforgettable scene 

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:09.720
from the first "Anchorman," Brian Fantana&nbsp;
shares his secret to success with women,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:14.700
revealing his off-brand cologne "Sex Panther."&nbsp;
So, obviously, when it came to making a sequel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:19.067
to "Anchorman," the writers wanted to develop a&nbsp;
similar scene in hopes of recreating the magic.

00:04:19.067 --> 00:04:23.820
For "Anchorman 2," Fantana's cupboard gets much&nbsp;
larger than holding one bottle of cologne. Rather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:27.600
he has an entire shrine of condoms and&nbsp;
lubricants that he shares with the rest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:31.380
of the Channel 4 News team. The scene is a&nbsp;
hysterical moment during the finalized film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:34.800
but the bloopers take it up another&nbsp;
notch. Analyzing different condoms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:39.019
the actors improvise an assortment of&nbsp;
lines around their individual purposes.

00:04:39.019 --> 00:04:43.800
As Veronica Corningstone, Christina Applegate&nbsp;
plays the even-tempered professional thrown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:47.580
into the clown show known as Channel 4&nbsp;
News. Burgundy and crew grow to resent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:50.940
the anchorwoman stepping into a role&nbsp;
beside them, so they hatch a plan to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:54.840
ruin her career. This is showcased when the&nbsp;
news team attempts to throw her off by making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:59.880
faces and gestures while she's live on air —&nbsp;
but Corningstone remains completely unfazed.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.800
"I'm Veronica Corningstone, and&nbsp;
thanks for stopping by San Diego."

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:07.980
However, as easy as Applegate's&nbsp;
character made it look in the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:12.180
the actress had a much harder time keeping a&nbsp;
straight face. One blooper depicts Applegate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:16.256
giggling as Paul Rudd and David Koechner&nbsp;
taunt her while she reads her news report.

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:19.980
As weatherman Brick Tamland,&nbsp;
Steve Carell quickly became a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:23.515
fan favorite and delivered some of the most&nbsp;
quotable lines in the "Anchorman" movies.

00:05:23.515 --> 00:05:24.655
"I love lamp."

00:05:24.655 --> 00:05:29.079
Rudd admitted in an interview with GQ that one&nbsp;
scene with Carell made him "laugh the hardest."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.079 --> 00:05:32.880
The moment in question involved Carell's&nbsp;
character eating a wrap for lunch. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:37.691
the meal consisted of a used coffee filter wrapped&nbsp;
around a cigarette butt and loose change.  
 &nbsp;

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:42.180
Aside from Ferrell, the next biggest name on&nbsp;
screen in "Anchorman" was Paul Rudd. In an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:46.680
interview with GQ, Rudd pointed to one particular&nbsp;
scene when he struggled to hold it together. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:50.700
takes place after the Channel 4 News team&nbsp;
gets into a street fight with a rival crew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:55.772
from another news channel. Licking their wounds in&nbsp;
Ron Burgundy's office, Ferrell delivers the line,

00:05:55.772 --> 00:05:58.400
"I'm proud of you fellas. You&nbsp;
all kept your head on a swivel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:01.560
And that's what you got to do when you&nbsp;
find yourself in a vicious cockfight."

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:04.200
The improvised bit proved too&nbsp;
much for the veteran Rudd,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:06.473
as he remembers breaking character and laughing.

00:06:06.473 --> 00:06:10.390
"That's not in the script or anything&nbsp;
and I was like, 'Took me by surprise.'"

00:06:10.390 --> 00:06:13.320
Meanwhile, the blooper reel reveals&nbsp;
that Ferrell also couldn't help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:15.478
but laugh at his own impromptu joke.

00:06:15.478 --> 00:06:17.666
David Koechner's performance as Champ Kind 

00:06:17.666 --> 00:06:22.740
rounded out the fantastic four of Channel 4, as he&nbsp;
uttered some of the crew's most obscene comments.

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:24.900
"I'm all about having fun. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:29.680
know, get a couple cocktails in me.&nbsp;
Start a fire in someone's kitchen."

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:32.940
Surely, Koechner enjoyed playing the&nbsp;
role, as Champ resembles other characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:36.480
he has played. However, when it came to&nbsp;
returning to do the "Anchorman" sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:39.600
the actor may have gotten a little&nbsp;
too excited. In a scene where Champ&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:43.996
Kind embraces his old friend Ron Burgundy,&nbsp;
the hug gets awkward and a tad aggressive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.996 --> 00:06:48.556
The moment is even funnier in the outtakes, as&nbsp;
Koechner takes the uncomfortable moment further.

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:52.920
Over the course of two movies, there is enough&nbsp;
"Anchorman" to leave everyone's sides hurting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:57.242
from laughter. However, Rudd found one scene&nbsp;
so funny that he couldn't even get through it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.242 --> 00:07:01.440
During an interview with The Ringer, he mentions&nbsp;
the scene from the first film where Ferrell says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:05.460
"Milk was a bad choice," which was nearly&nbsp;
ruined by laughter. But it was a joke from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:08.880
Ferrell in the sequel that really took the&nbsp;
cake for Rudd. During the scene in question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:12.000
Burgundy is so depressed from&nbsp;
losing everything that he remarks:

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:15.540
"The other day I pleasured myself&nbsp;
to the image of Mrs. Buttersworth."

00:07:15.767 --> 00:07:19.007
Thankfully, this hilarious moment&nbsp;
also made it into the blooper reels.
 &nbsp;

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:23.640
The hilarity continued in the "Anchorman 2"&nbsp;
scene in which the Channel 4 News team arrived&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:28.260
at Burgundy's house to cheer him up. Wrapped in a&nbsp;
blanket and suffering from depression, Ferrell's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.260 --> 00:07:32.580
character is completely over-the-top with his&nbsp;
self-depreciation, much to the frustration of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:36.720
friends. However, for the actors, it was easily&nbsp;
one of the most entertaining days of filming.

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:40.740
During one outtake from the scene, Burgundy&nbsp;
compares his suffering to that of the Elephant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:44.402
Man. He then does an impression of the&nbsp;
Elephant Man, which instantly breaks Rudd.

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:49.260
Ferrell has spent his entire career surrounded&nbsp;
by some of Hollywood's funniest people. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:52.860
actor famously makes everybody around him&nbsp;
break character while he improvises and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:56.340
takes things to unexpected levels. It&nbsp;
is only fair that once in a blue moon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.340 --> 00:08:00.840
the comedic dynamo loses control and ruins&nbsp;
a scene by laughing. However, in "Anchorman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:05.426
2," Ferrell is bested by an actor that you would&nbsp;
least suspect to make him laugh, Harrison Ford.

00:08:05.426 --> 00:08:09.720
In the movie, Ford plays a superstar anchorman&nbsp;
who does not have a soft spot for Burgundy. During&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:13.080
one of the film's few serious moments,&nbsp;
when Ford's character talks about being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:17.275
responsible for the death of a window washer,&nbsp;
Ferrell can't help but burst into laughter. 

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:21.900
One of the funniest gags from "Anchorman 2,"&nbsp;
involved Champ Kind leaving the news business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:25.560
behind and opening a fried chicken&nbsp;
restaurant. The movie explains how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:29.880
Kind faked an accident at work and used the&nbsp;
settlement money to open his joint. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:34.442
in an effort to save money, Koechner's character&nbsp;
explains that he uses bats instead of poultry.

00:08:34.442 --> 00:08:37.620
Amusingly, Koechner was on top of&nbsp;
using his character's situation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:41.520
and improvised many jokes about&nbsp;
using other possible meats. One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:45.000
outtake finds Ferrell cracking up&nbsp;
after Koechner delivers this line:

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:48.254
"I figure after it's been in a&nbsp;
deep fryer, it's no longer human."

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:52.260
Among the best parts of watching the&nbsp;
"Anchorman" movies is catching the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:56.940
never-ending assortment of celebrity cameos.&nbsp;
However, two cameos proved perfectly fitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.940 --> 00:09:00.540
considering Ferrell and McKay shared a&nbsp;
long history of collaborating with them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:04.800
on "Saturday Night Live." Tina Fey and Amy&nbsp;
Poehler seamlessly entered their way into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:09.180
the news team royal rumble in "Anchorman&nbsp;
2" representing an all-female news crew.

00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:13.860
Poehler and Fey together in front of a camera&nbsp;
equals comedy magic. There are multiple bloopers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:18.720
and outtakes showcasing the non-stop giggling and&nbsp;
joking the two women brought to the set. Fey and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:23.580
Poehler also improvised many hilarious lines&nbsp;
during their short appearance, including this gem:

00:09:23.784 --> 00:09:25.644
"I'll ride your mustache all the way to hell."

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:31.200
In a hilarious outtake from"Anchorman 2," Ferrell&nbsp;
is guilty of blurting out the wrong cast member's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:35.640
name when sharing a scene with Koechner. Ferrell&nbsp;
incidentally calls Koechner "Brick," which happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:39.780
to be the first name of Steve Carell's character.&nbsp;
Where most actors would take the simple mistake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:44.134
in stride, Koechner hilariously punishes&nbsp;
Ferrell by telling him, "Brick's dead!" 
 &nbsp;

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:48.000
The "Anchorman" movies are so funny that even&nbsp;
when the actors are keeping to the script,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:52.140
they still can't help but laugh. For instance,&nbsp;
in "Anchorman 2," Rudd's character shares an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.140 --> 00:09:56.100
upcoming expose about "the powers and&nbsp;
mysteries of the human vagina," which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.100 --> 00:09:59.880
proves to be too much for Ferrell in an&nbsp;
outtake. Amusingly, Rudd does not let up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:03.603
calling it an "explosive piece," which&nbsp;
almost puts Ferrell on the floor.
 &nbsp;

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:08.580
In "Anchorman 2", Steve Carell played the Channel&nbsp;
4 weatherman with as much ridiculousness as he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:12.480
did the first time around. Between delivering&nbsp;
a eulogy at his own funeral to getting married&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:16.200
to the love of his life, Brick also had a&nbsp;
far more significant role in the sequel.

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:21.900
"Brick was a great man and I miss him so much.&nbsp;
And I will not rest until I find his killer."

00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:26.100
However, that did not mean that he was not&nbsp;
up to his old gags. Sure, Carell's character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:30.000
doesn't eat another coffee filter, but the&nbsp;
bloopers show he had just as many struggles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:34.260
chowing down food. For instance, one outtake&nbsp;
shows him spitting beer all over himself.

00:10:34.860 --> 00:10:38.820
A significant challenge that many movie&nbsp;
productions face when filming outside of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.820 --> 00:10:43.200
studio, is excessive noise — particularly,&nbsp;
airplanes and helicopters. Thankfully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:46.020
when a movie like "Anchorman" is&nbsp;
fully loaded with comedic talent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.020 --> 00:10:49.134
even an unforeseen obstruction&nbsp;
can become a side-splitting joke.

00:10:49.134 --> 00:10:53.220
Hilariously, when a loud helicopter ruined&nbsp;
a scene for "Anchorman," Ferrell joked that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.220 --> 00:10:56.940
his personal transport had arrived. Quipping&nbsp;
that he has a helicopter to travel him around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.940 --> 00:11:01.860
San Diego is an ongoing joke for Burgundy. The&nbsp;
first time audiences met the local anchorman at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.860 --> 00:11:06.701
the beginning of the first film in 2004, he&nbsp;
stepped out of a "Channel 4 News" chopper. 

00:11:06.701 --> 00:11:10.080
In an additional scene involving Champ&nbsp;
Kind's unorthodox fried chicken restaurant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:14.400
David Koechner gets beaten by his own joke&nbsp;
when Rudd delivers a line that completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:17.820
catches him off guard. In the outtake,&nbsp;
Koechner's character asks his friend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.820 --> 00:11:21.840
to "Name a meat," to which Fantana replies,&nbsp;
"chicken." Out of all the potential answers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:26.335
Koechner was obviously not expecting that&nbsp;
one, and instantaneously burst into laughter.

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:30.840
One of the most awkward parts about being an&nbsp;
actor or actress must be sharing extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.840 --> 00:11:34.260
intimate scenes with a co-star. So,&nbsp;
we imagine it was almost impossible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.260 --> 00:11:38.734
for Christina Applegate to keep a straight face&nbsp;
during her "Anchorman" bed scene with Ferrell.

00:11:38.734 --> 00:11:42.660
It's particularly hard to forget the part of&nbsp;
the scene in which Burgundy and Applegate's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.660 --> 00:11:46.980
Veronica Corningstone make out. After all,&nbsp;
aggressive biting of lips and far too much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.980 --> 00:11:51.300
tongue transformed a standard kissing session&nbsp;
into comedy gold for audiences. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:55.740
Ferrell's goofball antics had Applegate cracking&nbsp;
up, as seen in the film's blooper reel.  
 &nbsp;

00:11:56.460 --> 00:11:58.560
Not many performers can match Carell's level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:01.200
of hilarity — seven seasons&nbsp;
on "The Office" proved that.

00:12:04.195 --> 00:12:05.700
"It's Michael Scott."

00:12:05.700 --> 00:12:09.060
However, when seeking someone to&nbsp;
play opposite Carell for "Anchorman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.060 --> 00:12:12.540
2," McKay knocked it out of the park&nbsp;
by hiring "Saturday Night Live" alum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.540 --> 00:12:16.080
Kristen Wiig. Wiig and Carell delivered&nbsp;
an onscreen connection that was perfect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:20.700
and better described as pure awkwardness.&nbsp;
What makes their off-beat romance even more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:24.840
entertaining is the fact that much of their time&nbsp;
on-screen together was completely improvised.

00:12:24.840 --> 00:12:28.320
The bloopers released for "Anchorman&nbsp;
2" are packed to the brim with outtakes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:32.400
featuring Carell and Wiig making each other&nbsp;
laugh. That said, one scene between the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:37.140
goes completely off the rails when Carell&nbsp;
tells Wiig that his favorite food is pee. 
 &nbsp;

00:12:37.140 --> 00:12:41.940
Ron Burgundy's turnaround in "Anchorman 2" occurs&nbsp;
when he realizes that he wants to deliver America&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.940 --> 00:12:46.680
the news that they want to hear — like why the&nbsp;
USA is the greatest place on Earth. Before that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.680 --> 00:12:50.280
however, Burgundy was in serious trouble&nbsp;
of finding his voice while in the national&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.280 --> 00:12:54.360
spotlight. The scene between Ferrell and&nbsp;
Dylan Baker, who plays Burgundy's boss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:59.040
is vital to pushing the movie forward — and&nbsp;
resulted in one of the movie's greatest bloopers.

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:02.820
In the scene, Baker's character gets mad&nbsp;
at Burgundy's ideas and finally says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.820 --> 00:13:06.600
"That's not news!" Ferrell is supposed to&nbsp;
respond with a line about grabbing popcorn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:10.500
and watching the end of the world, but breaks&nbsp;
character before he can even get the words out.
 &nbsp;

00:13:11.220 --> 00:13:15.420
There is another "Anchorman" star that does&nbsp;
not get enough credit: Burgundy's loyal pet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.420 --> 00:13:19.800
pup Baxter. Surprisingly, this furry actor&nbsp;
was almost the reason the beloved comedy film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.800 --> 00:13:24.060
received a more mature rating than PG-13.&nbsp;
Baxter appears in much of the first film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.060 --> 00:13:27.420
including one particularly memorable&nbsp;
scene where he's kicked off a bridge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.420 --> 00:13:31.740
by Jack Black. The dog plays a pivotal&nbsp;
role in the plot and in Burgundy's heart.

00:13:34.200 --> 00:13:36.840
"You know how to cut to the core of me, Baxter."

00:13:36.840 --> 00:13:40.080
However, McKay revealed in the&nbsp;
director's commentary that Baxter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.080 --> 00:13:44.280
nearly earned "Anchorman" an R rating. The&nbsp;
pup had a habit of revealing its genitalia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.280 --> 00:13:48.720
and his parts can be seen throughout the film.&nbsp;
Apparently, the Motion Picture Association of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:53.580
America abides by a strict rule stating that a&nbsp;
movie will get an instant R for more than five&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.580 --> 00:13:58.680
seconds of close-up images of male genitalia —&nbsp;
and that includes animals! So McKay was forced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.680 --> 00:14:03.274
to edit out several shots of the cute,&nbsp;
fluffy pup and his overly apparent parts.

